{
  "answer": "Supervised learning uses labeled data and algorithms to train models to predict outcomes or classify information accurately. In contrast, unsupervised learning uses unlabeled data to allow algorithms to discover hidden patterns, structures, or groupings without human intervention.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "key_points": [
    "Supervised learning requires labeled data; unsupervised learning uses unlabeled data.",
    "Supervised learning focuses on prediction and classification; unsupervised learning focuses on pattern recognition and clustering.",
    "Supervised models require explicit target outputs during training; unsupervised models explore the data autonomously."
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "Overfitting occurs when a machine learning model learns the training data too well, capturing its noise and random fluctuations rather than the underlying pattern, which leads to poor generalization on unseen data. One way to reduce it is through regularization (such as L1 or L2 regularization), which adds a penalty to the loss function for large model weights.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "key_points": [
    "Learns training noise and details instead of general patterns",
    "Results in poor performance on new, unseen data",
    "Can be reduced using regularization techniques like L1 or L2"
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "Gradient descent is an iterative optimization algorithm used to minimize a loss function by moving in the direction of the steepest descent, defined by the negative gradient. It is used in training neural networks to systematically adjust the network's weights and biases, thereby reducing prediction errors and improving the model's accuracy over time.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "key_points": [
    "Iterative optimization algorithm",
    "Minimizes a loss or cost function",
    "Uses the gradient to determine the direction of steepest descent",
    "Adjusts neural network weights and biases to reduce error"
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "Precision measures the accuracy of positive predictions, specifically the ratio of true positives to all predicted positives, answering 'how many of the selected items are relevant?'. Recall, also known as sensitivity, measures the ability to find all actual positive instances, calculated as the ratio of true positives to all actual positives, answering 'how many of the relevant items are selected?'.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "key_points": [
    "Precision focuses on the quality of positive predictions (True Positives / (True Positives + False Positives)).",
    "Recall focuses on the quantity of actual positives detected (True Positives / (True Positives + False Negatives)).",
    "There is typically a trade-off between precision and recall depending on the classification threshold."
  ]
}

{
  "answer": "Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) is a technique that enhances large language models by retrieving relevant information from an external knowledge base before generating a response. It is useful because it reduces hallucinations, provides up-to-date information, and grounds the model's outputs in verifiable sources without requiring full retraining.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "key_points": [
    "Combines information retrieval with text generation",
    "Queries external knowledge bases for context",
    "Reduces hallucinations and factual errors",
    "Allows access to private or up-to-date data"
  ]
}
